1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,096
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,096 --> 00:00:08,978
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:08,978 --> 00:00:13,700
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,619
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,619 --> 00:00:24,009
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:29,389
On the day, the book is up 205 dollars.

7
00:00:29,389 --> 00:00:32,392
Target (TGT) leads, +0.6 percent.

8
00:00:32,392 --> 00:00:34,394
3M (MMM) lags at -0.3.

9
00:00:34,394 --> 00:00:39,308
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:39,308 --> 00:00:41,926
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:41,926 --> 00:00:45,578
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:45,578 --> 00:00:48,610
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:54,122
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:03,313
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:03,313 --> 00:01:10,112
10 positions, led by Target, ticker TGT, Dollar Tree, ticker DLTR, 3M, ticker MMM.

16
00:01:10,112 --> 00:01:21,387
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:27,406
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:27,406 --> 00:01:29,749
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:29,749 --> 00:01:40,042
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:40,042 --> 00:01:43,891
Inside the book today: 7 positions up, 3 down.

21
00:01:43,891 --> 00:01:46,234
Best, DLTR at +1.47 percent.

22
00:01:46,234 --> 00:01:47,908
Worst, MMM at -0.26.

23
00:01:47,908 --> 00:01:52,762
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:01:52,762 --> 00:01:59,791
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.83% per trade over 1311 graded positions.

25
00:01:59,791 --> 00:02:06,653
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

26
00:02:06,653 --> 00:02:11,674
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:11,674 --> 00:02:23,556
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 1 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:27,811
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:27,811 --> 00:02:37,259
Day 95 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:37,259 --> 00:02:43,653
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 108,401.

31
00:02:43,653 --> 00:02:49,284
Then the index fund, in gray, 7.0 thousand back at 101,387.

32
00:02:49,284 --> 00:02:56,633
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 8.8 thousand back at 99,633.

33
00:02:56,633 --> 00:02:59,305
The race has no finish line.

34
00:02:59,305 --> 00:03:03,695
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:03,695 --> 00:03:08,658
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:08,658 --> 00:03:10,567
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:10,567 --> 00:03:19,437
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:28,720
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:30,957
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:30,957 --> 00:03:33,939
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
